>> title: Mustard Greens and Tofu Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: mustard greens, tofu, pork, ginger, cantonese, quick soup, weeknight, stovetop
>> counters: One Pot
>> aka: 芥菜豆腐瘦肉湯, gaai coi dau fu sau juk tong, gai choi dau fu sau yuk tong, mustard greens and tofu soup, gai choy and tofu soup, 滾湯, gwan tong
>> servings: 4
>> time: 30 min
>> slack: narrow — the greens go from bitter-green to olive and slippery in about fifteen minutes, and the bitterness is the bowl
>> washing-up: the soup pot, a bowl to dress the pork in

>> step: Bitter on purpose. The ginger fried first makes 芥菜 bearable rather than covering it, and the water boils first.
A weeknight bowl, and a bitter one on purpose. 芥菜 is meant to taste of itself; the ginger fried first is what makes that bearable, not what covers it up.

>> step: fry the ginger in oil, 2 min
Fry @ginger{5%slices}(unpeeled, bruised with the flat of a knife) in @vegetable oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) in a #soup pot{} ~fry{2%min}, until the edges colour.

>> step: boil 10 min with the greens
Boil @&(~1)fried ginger{} with @water{6%cups}(1.4 L) and @mustard greens{1%lb}(450 g; gaai coi, cut into lengths — stems in first, leaves after) ~boil{10%min}.

>> step: slice the pork thin and dress it
Slice @lean pork{1/4%lb}(115 g) thin across the grain and turn it with @cornstarch{1%tsp} and @light soy sauce{2%tsp}(10 mL).

>> step: stir the pork and the tofu in, 4 min
Stir @&(~1)dressed pork{} and @firm tofu{10%oz}(280 g; in thick slabs, slid in rather than dropped) into @&(~2)soup{} and ~stir{4%min}, until the pork has just turned.

>> step: season at the end
Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp} and @white pepper{1/4%tsp}.
